  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The velocity $v,$ in meters per second, of a particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=\u27e8-12\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t,\\,8\\sqrt{t}\u27e9,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer to one decimal place.",
  "worked_solution": "Newton\u2019s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-12\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\sqrt{t})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{12}{t},\\,\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t}}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot \u27e8-\\frac{12}{t},\\,\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{24}{t},\\,\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{t}}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =\u27e8-\\frac{24}{4},\\,\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-6,\\,4\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(4)| & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{(4)}^{2}} \\\\  & \u22487.2\\,\\text{N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
